---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navigation from '@/components/Navigation.astro';
import { Footer } from '@/components/Footer';
import { ConsentBanner } from '@/components/ConsentBanner';
import { firstVisitSchema } from '@/lib/schemas';

import img1Raw from "@/assets/clinic/sciatica-assessment.jpeg";
import img2Raw from "@/assets/conditions/back-massage.jpg";
import img3Raw from "@/assets/clinic/treatment-session.webp";
import img4Raw from "@/assets/clinic/stretching-for-neck-pain.jpg";
import img5Raw from "@/assets/clinic/shoulder-assessment-movement.png";
---

<BaseLayout 
  title="What a Thorough Assessment Involves | Scandinavian Clinic"
  description="What a thorough assessment actually includes: a detailed history, movement testing, joint testing, and muscle evaluation."
  robots="noindex, nofollow"
  jsonLd={firstVisitSchema}
>
  <div class="min-h-screen">
    <Navigation />
    
    <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 md:py-20 prose prose-lg prose-primary dark:prose-invert">
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-4">What a Thorough Assessment Involves</h1>
      
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-6">What does a thorough assessment actually include?</h2>
      
      <p>Four parts: a detailed history, an examination of how you move — where range is lost and where the two sides no longer match, testing of individual joints and spinal segments, and evaluation of the muscles as a working system. Each finding is then tested against your symptoms, because a finding only matters if it relates to what you came in with. Treatment follows from what the examination establishes.</p>
      
      <p class="font-medium">
        <a href="https://booking.scandinavianclinic.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="booking-link underline text-accent hover:text-primary transition-colors">Book Your Assessment</a> · <a href="/how-recovery-works/why-pain-keeps-coming-back" class="underline hover:text-accent transition-colors">Why pain keeps coming back &rarr;</a>
      </p>

      <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold mb-6">What happens on your first visit?</h2>
      <p>Ideally, book 60 minutes for an initial visit, so there's time for the full assessment and treatment. Arrive 10 minutes early to complete the intake form.</p>
      <p>Bring a tank top and shorts. The examination depends on being able to see your spine, posture and joint movement directly. If you have previous imaging or diagnostic reports, bring them — not required, but useful context.</p>
      <p>The visit begins with a conversation, moves to the physical examination, and usually ends with treatment based on what was found. What each of those involves is described below.</p>

      <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold mb-6">The order the work follows</h2>
      <p>Every condition treated here usually runs through the same four steps. The order isn't arbitrary — each step makes the next one possible.</p>
      
      <p><strong>1. Find out which areas have stopped doing their share.</strong> When one region stops contributing, the ones beside it take on the work — and that is often where the pain shows up. So the examination covers the whole chain, not only the painful region, and each finding is tested against your symptoms.</p>
      
      <p><strong>2. Restore movement where it's restricted.</strong> A pattern can't change while the mechanics prevent it. Gentle, low-velocity mobilisation to joints and segments that have lost their normal movement.</p>
      
      <p><strong>3. Settle the muscles doing too much, before anything is strengthened.</strong> Painful muscles are usually already overworking rather than weak. Settling them is what makes the underworking ones trainable.</p>
      
      <p><strong>4. Retrain the movement itself.</strong> Not isolated muscles — whole patterns, carrying real weight, in the movements your day actually contains.</p>
      
      <p>Strengthening a movement that's already going wrong makes it stronger, not better. That's why step 4 comes last.</p>

      <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold mb-6">Step 1: The clinical history</h2>
      
      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg not-prose">
        <Image src={img1Raw} alt="The clinical history" class="w-full h-auto object-cover max-h-[500px]" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
      </div>

      <p>Your first visit begins with a conversation, not a table.</p>
      <p>The history covers when the symptoms started, what movements or situations bring them on, what makes them better or worse, how they affect your day, and what has already been tried. Previous imaging and medical records add useful context if you have them.</p>
      <p>It does two jobs. It narrows the likely mechanical sources before the physical examination begins, and it establishes what you actually need — treatment for a trail runner with recurring hip pain is planned differently from treatment for someone who can't sit through a workday.</p>
      <p>Where treatment has been tried before without lasting results, the history often surfaces the pattern of how the pain returns and what it responds to, which points toward factors not previously identified.</p>

      <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold mb-6">Step 2: The physical examination</h2>
      
      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">What the examination is looking for</h3>
      <p>For most musculoskeletal pain there is no single damaged structure to find (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(18)30480-X" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">Hartvigsen et al., <em>The Lancet</em>, 2018</a>). What can be identified is a mechanical pattern — which regions have stopped contributing, which are doing more work than they should, and which movements reproduce the symptom. That pattern is established by testing how you actually move.</p>
      <p>The body moves symmetrically and efficiently when it can, and it works as one connected system. When one side moves less than the other, when a segment is restricted, or when a muscle group is doing work its counterpart should share, the body compensates.</p>
      <p>Those compensations — asymmetries and departures from efficient movement — are what the examination looks for, because they indicate where the problem is being produced rather than only where it's felt.</p>
      <p>The examination reads three layers together. No single layer answers on its own; the picture comes from how the findings line up.</p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">How is movement tested?</h3>
      
      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg not-prose">
        <Image src={img2Raw} alt="Movement testing" class="w-full h-auto object-cover max-h-[500px]" />
      </div>

      <p>Movement testing looks at how you move as a whole — bending, turning, reaching — read against two references: the range the movement should have, and the symmetry between your two sides.</p>
      <p>Asymmetry is the key indicator. A movement that's restricted or uneven side to side shows where the body has adapted, often well away from the spot that hurts. And a movement that reproduces your familiar pain shows which structures are involved — a controlled way of letting the pain point to its own source.</p>
      <p>This is why movement testing comes first. It establishes where to look more closely in the joint and muscle testing that follows.</p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">How are joints tested?</h3>
      
      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg not-prose">
        <Image src={img3Raw} alt="Segmental joint testing" class="w-full h-auto object-cover max-h-[500px]" />
      </div>

      <p>The spine is not one joint but a chain of segments, each contributing its own share of movement. The segments are tested individually — how each one moves, and whether it moves the same to the left as to the right.</p>
      <p>A segment that moves freely one way but is restricted the other is an imbalance, and it's treatable, because the restriction sits in the tissues around it: the joint, the ligaments, and the muscles that control it. Local restrictions like these link into chain reactions through the movement system, which is one reason a restricted segment can produce symptoms a long way from itself.</p>
      <p>This is detail that whole-body movement testing can't reach. Two people with the same back pain can have restrictions at completely different segments — which is why the same treatment can help one and do little for the other.</p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">How are muscles tested?</h3>
      
      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg not-prose">
        <Image src={img4Raw} alt="Muscle system evaluation" class="w-full h-auto object-cover max-h-[500px]" />
      </div>

      <p>Muscles are tested as a system, alongside the movement and joint findings, rather than as isolated parts. What's assessed is tone, tightness, and the balance between groups: which muscles are overworking, which are underactive, and how that fits the restrictions already found.</p>
      <p>Certain muscles tend to tighten while their opposites become underactive, in patterns predictable enough to be examined for directly. The deep stabilising muscles are also assessed for timing rather than strength — in people with low back pain, the deep abdominal wall has been shown to activate later than it should relative to movement (<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9018337/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">Hodges & Richardson, <em>Spine</em>, 1996</a>).</p>
      <p>This matters because the muscle findings direct the treatment. A muscle held in chronic tension needs release; its underactive counterpart needs activation and training. Treating one without the other tends to give short-lived results, because the imbalance producing the tension is still there.</p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Testing findings against your symptoms</h3>
      
      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg not-prose">
        <Image src={img5Raw} alt="Testing findings against symptoms" class="w-full h-auto object-cover max-h-[500px]" />
      </div>

      <p>An examination will always turn up findings. A stiff segment, a tight muscle, an asymmetry — most people have several, and many of them are incidental.</p>
      <p>So each finding is tested against your symptoms: does provoking it reproduce your familiar pain, does relieving it change what you feel, does it fit the pattern the history described? A finding that doesn't connect to your symptoms is noted and set aside.</p>
      <p>This step is what separates a list of findings from an explanation.</p>

      <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold mb-6">Step 3: How treatment begins</h2>
      <p>Treatment follows the findings, and usually begins in the same visit.</p>
      <p>What the examination found is explained in plain language first. Techniques are then chosen to match: soft-tissue work including fascia, joint mobilisation for restricted segments, specific stretching, post-isometric relaxation — a gentle contraction held against light resistance, then release and a soft stretch as the muscle lets go — and neuromuscular work to restore movement control.</p>
      <p>Some acute problems settle in two or three sessions. Patterns built up over years are changed step by step, each session building on the last. Mild soreness for a day or two afterwards is common.</p>
      <p>You'll be given a realistic plan based on what was found and what you need, rather than a standard package.</p>

      <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold mb-6">Step 4: Training, and keeping the change</h2>
      <p>Lasting results depend on what happens between sessions.</p>
      <p>As treatment restores movement, home exercises are added — matched to your specific findings, with instruction on performing them correctly, alongside practical guidance on the positions and habits that contributed in the first place.</p>
      <p>For many people this includes Dynamic Neuromuscular Stabilisation: training the deep stabilising system of the trunk — the diaphragm, pelvic floor and abdominal wall working together — using positions from infant motor development, because these make the correct coordination happen without you having to think about it. <a href="/how-recovery-works/core-stability-and-breathing" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">How core stability and breathing work &rarr;</a></p>
      <p>Ten to fifteen minutes of daily practice is usually what makes a pattern automatic. The aim throughout is that you leave understanding what was producing the problem, with the means to keep it from returning.</p>

      <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold mb-6">What happens after the first visit?</h2>
      <p>The assessment isn't finished when the first appointment ends. It continues as the response to treatment shows what was accurate and what needs revising.</p>
      <p>A second visit usually begins by checking what changed — which findings have settled, which haven't, and how the home exercises felt. Treatment is adjusted from there. Where something hasn't responded the way the examination predicted, it's re-examined rather than simply repeated.</p>
      <p>How far apart visits are spaced depends on the problem. Acute problems are often seen closer together at first. Long-standing patterns are usually spaced further apart, so there's time between sessions for the training to take effect.</p>

      <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold mb-6">Is the assessment covered by insurance?</h2>
      <p><strong>Extended health:</strong> most BC plans cover Registered Massage Therapy. You'll receive an official RMT receipt to submit for reimbursement.</p>
      <p><strong>ICBC, WorkSafeBC, RCMP, MSP-exempt programs:</strong> do not book online — call <strong><a href="tel:+16049264883" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">(604) 926-4883</a></strong> so the correct intake procedure is followed.</p>
      <p><strong>Cancellations:</strong> 24 hours' notice is required; late changes are subject to the full session fee.</p>
      <p><a href="/fees" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">Fees and insurance &rarr;</a></p>

      <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold mb-6">The Scandinavian Clinic</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Every treatment begins with the assessment described on this page.</strong> Not with assumptions about where it hurts.</li>
        <li><strong>Swedish-trained orthopaedic manual therapy.</strong> Five years of university-level training in Sweden, with a strong emphasis on clinical reasoning and biomechanical assessment.</li>
        <li><strong>Czech-trained in Dynamic Neuromuscular Stabilisation</strong>, at the Prague School of Rehabilitation Medicine.</li>
        <li><strong>Covered by most extended health plans.</strong> As a Registered Massage Therapist, Eva's treatments are eligible for extended health benefits.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Eva Andersson has practised in North Vancouver since 1994. She lives on the North Shore, and spends her own time hiking, biking, paddling and skiing. <a href="/about-me" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">More about Eva &rarr;</a></p>

      <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-medium mt-0 mb-2">Do I need a doctor's referral?</h3>
          <p class="mt-0">No. You can book directly with a Registered Massage Therapist in BC. If your visit falls under ICBC, WorkSafeBC or a similar programme, call the clinic first so intake is handled correctly.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-medium mt-0 mb-2">Does the assessment hurt?</h3>
          <p class="mt-0">The tests are controlled and stay within your comfort. Some deliberately reproduce your familiar pain briefly — that response is itself a finding, because it shows which structures are involved.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-medium mt-0 mb-2">Why do I need to change into shorts and a tank top?</h3>
          <p class="mt-0">The examination depends on seeing your spine, posture and joint movement directly. Loose or heavy clothing hides exactly the asymmetries the assessment is designed to find.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-medium mt-0 mb-2">Will I be treated on the first visit?</h3>
          <p class="mt-0">Usually. A 60-minute visit has time for the history, the examination and initial treatment based on the findings. The assessment continues to be refined over following sessions.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-medium mt-0 mb-2">Do I need a scan first?</h3>
          <p class="mt-0">Usually not. In people with no symptoms at all, disc degeneration is present in 37% of 20-year-olds and 96% of 80-year-olds, and disc bulges in 30% and 84% (<a href="https://www.ajnr.org/content/36/4/811" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">Brinjikji et al., 2015</a>) — so a scan often reports changes that aren't causing your symptoms, and guidelines advise against routine early imaging for ordinary back pain (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(18)30489-6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">Foster et al., 2018</a>). Where red flags are present or recovery stalls, imaging becomes useful and you'll be advised to see your family physician.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-medium mt-0 mb-2">I've tried treatment elsewhere and the pain came back. Is this different?</h3>
          <p class="mt-0">The assessment often identifies factors not previously looked at — a restricted spinal segment, a muscle imbalance, a breathing or stabilising pattern — which then become the focus. <a href="/how-recovery-works/why-pain-keeps-coming-back" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">More on why pain returns &rarr;</a></p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-medium mt-0 mb-2">How many sessions will I need?</h3>
          <p class="mt-0">It depends on the findings. Some acute problems settle in two or three; patterns built over years are changed gradually. You'll get a realistic estimate after the examination.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-medium mt-0 mb-2">Is this massage therapy or something else?</h3>
          <p class="mt-0">It is Registered Massage Therapy. Assessment, manual treatment, joint mobilisation and rehabilitation exercise all fall within RMT scope in BC, and treatments are covered as RMT by most extended health plans.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <hr class="my-12 border-border" />

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold mb-6">Schedule Your Visit</h2>
      <p class="font-medium text-lg">
        <a href="https://booking.scandinavianclinic.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="booking-link underline text-accent hover:text-primary transition-colors">Book Your Assessment</a>
      </p>
      <p>Suite 202 – 101 West 16th Street, North Vancouver · <a href="tel:+16049264883" class="text-accent hover:text-primary underline">(604) 926-4883</a></p>

      <div class="mt-16 text-sm text-muted-foreground border-t border-border pt-8">
        <h3 class="font-semibold mb-4 text-foreground">References</h3>
        <ul class="list-none pl-0 space-y-2">
          <li class="pl-0">Hartvigsen J, Hancock MJ, Kongsted A, et al. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(18)30480-X" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">What low back pain is and why we need to pay attention.</a> <em>The Lancet.</em> 2018;391(10137):2356–2367.</li>
          <li class="pl-0">Brinjikji W, Luetmer PH, Comstock B, et al. <a href="https://www.ajnr.org/content/36/4/811" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">Systematic literature review of imaging features of spinal degeneration in asymptomatic populations.</a> <em>AJNR Am J Neuroradiol.</em> 2015;36(4):811–816.</li>
          <li class="pl-0">Foster NE, Anema JR, Cherkin D, et al. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(18)30489-6" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">Prevention and treatment of low back pain: evidence, challenges, and promising directions.</a> <em>The Lancet.</em> 2018;391(10137):2368–2383.</li>
          <li class="pl-0">Hodges PW, Richardson CA. <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9018337/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">Inefficient muscular stabilization of the lumbar spine associated with low back pain: a motor control evaluation of transversus abdominis.</a> <em>Spine.</em> 1996;21(22):2640–2650.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="italic mt-6">Written by Eva Andersson, RMT · Last updated August 2026</p>
      </div>

    </main>
    
    <Footer />
    <ConsentBanner client:load />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
